<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip II of Macedon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 359–336 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> from 359 to 336 BCE and the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. He professionalised the Macedonian army, introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">sarissa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> and used diplomacy and conquest to dominate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a>. His reforms laid the groundwork for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>, and he was assassinated in 336 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Philip II of Macedon" width="318" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Mortel from Riyadh, Saudi Arabia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phillip_II,_king_of_Macedonia,_Roman_copy_of_Greek_original,_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek,_Copenhagen_(36420294055).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
